\id PHP - Restored Context Bible (RCB)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Philippians
\toc1 The Letter of Paul to the Assembly in Philippi
\toc2 Philippians
\toc3 Php
\mt1 Philippians

\imt Introduction
\ip This letter comes from Paul, writing under guard while he waits for a trial to be decided (1:7, 13, 20), to the assembly in Philippi, a city in the Roman province of Macedonia in what is now northern Greece. Timothy is named with him as sender. The date is somewhere in the middle of the first century. Where Paul was being held he never says — he mentions a praetorium (1:13) and passes on greetings from people in Caesar's household (4:22), and both of those existed in Rome and in the provincial capitals alike.
\ip Philippi was a Roman colony: a city settled with Roman veterans, governed on the Roman model, its official language Latin, its people holding Roman citizenship a long way from Rome. Rank, honor, patronage, and the public display of standing ordered ordinary life there. Much of what Paul writes cuts straight across that — a Messiah who goes down to a slave's execution, a command to count other people above yourself, and the claim that this community's real citizenship is registered somewhere else entirely.
\ip Paul had founded the community himself on his first crossing into Europe (Acts 16), and no other assembly had backed him as this one had. They sent him money more than once in the earliest days, and they had now sent a further gift by the hand of Epaphroditus, who fell dangerously ill after reaching Paul. This letter goes back with him. It carries thanks for the gift, an account of how the imprisonment has actually turned out, word of what Paul intends for Timothy, a hard warning about men pressing circumcision on Gentile believers, and an appeal to two women in the assembly who are at odds with each other.
\ip It is the warmest of Paul's surviving letters and the most insistent about joy — he uses the word and its relatives some sixteen times — and he writes it from a prison, with execution a live possibility, to a community under pressure of its own.

\c 1
\s1 Greeting
\p
\v 1 Paul and Timothy, slaves\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Greek \tl douloi\tl*. A \tl doulos\tl* had no legal standing and no will of his own over against his master. The word is also an honorific in Israel's scriptures, where Moses, David, and the prophets are each "the servant of the LORD" (Deuteronomy 34:5; Psalm 89:20). Both senses are live here.\f* of the Christ Jesus, to all the holy ones\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Greek \tl hagioi\tl*, "set-apart ones" — Israel's own covenant word for itself (Exodus 19:6; Deuteronomy 7:6).\f* in the Christ Jesus who are in Philippi\f + \fr 1:1 \ft A Roman colony in Macedonia, settled with Roman veterans and organized on the Roman pattern, with Latin as its official language and Roman citizenship as the standing its people prized.\f*, together with the overseers and the servants\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Greek \tl episkopoi\tl* and \tl diakonoi\tl* — those who watch over the community's affairs and those who do the serving. No other letter of Paul's names them in a greeting, and these are functions rather than the formal offices of later centuries. They may be the people who organized and sent the gift.\f*:
\v 2 Favor to you and peace\f + \fr 1:2 \ft Greek \tl eirēnē\tl*, standing for Hebrew \tl shalom\tl*: not the absence of trouble but wholeness and well-being.\f* from God our Father and the Lord Jesus the Christ.
\s1 Thanks and Prayer for a Partnership That Has Never Lapsed
\p
\v 3 I give thanks to my God at every remembrance of you,
\v 4 always, in every petition of mine for you all, making my petition with joy,
\v 5 because of your partnership\f + \fr 1:5 \ft Greek \tl koinōnia\tl*: a word for holding something in common — a business partnership, a joint venture in which both sides carry the stake and the risk. What it has meant in practice is spelled out at 4:15-16.\f* in the good news from the first day until now,
\v 6 confident of this very thing: that the one who began a good work in you will bring it to completion, right up to the day of the Christ Jesus\f + \fr 1:6 \ft "The day of the LORD" in the prophets — the day God intervenes to judge, vindicate, and put things right (Joel 2:1; Amos 5:18-20; Zephaniah 1:14-18) — named here as the day of the Messiah.\f*.
\v 7 It is only right for me to have this mind about you all, because I hold you in my heart — you who, both in my chains and in the defense and confirming of the good news, are all sharers with me in the favor \add [i.e. in the same gift God has handed me — the work, and the cost of it]\add*.
\v 8 For God is my witness how I long for you all in the inward parts of the Christ Jesus \add [i.e. with his own gut-deep affection at work in me, and not merely my own]\add*.
\p
\v 9 And this I pray: that your love may overflow still more and more, in full knowing and in every kind of perceiving,
\v 10 so that you may weigh out what really matters, and so be clear\f + \fr 1:10 \ft Greek \tl eilikrinēs\tl*, from the words for "sunlight" and "to judge": what can be held up in full daylight with nothing hidden in it.\f* and without stumbling for the day of the Christ,
\v 11 filled with the fruit of righteousness that comes through Jesus the Christ, to the glory and praise of God.
\s1 What the Chains Have Done for the Message
\p
\v 12 Now I want you to know, brothers, that what has happened to me has turned out rather for the advance of the good news:
\v 13 it has become plain, throughout the whole praetorium\f + \fr 1:13 \ft Greek \tl praitōrion\tl*: it can mean the emperor's guard at Rome, a provincial governor's residence and staff (Acts 23:35), or a military headquarters. Which is meant depends on where Paul was held, and he does not say.\f* and to all the rest, that my chains are in the Christ \add [i.e. that I am in here for him and for nothing else]\add*,
\v 14 and most of the brothers, having grown confident in the Lord because of my chains, dare much more to speak the word of God without fear.
\p
\v 15 Some, it is true, are proclaiming the Christ out of envy and strife, but others out of good will.
\v 16 These do it out of love, knowing that I am set here for the defense of the good news;
\v 17 those announce the Christ out of self-seeking rivalry\f + \fr 1:17 \ft Greek \tl eritheia\tl*: the word for canvassing and faction-building in the scramble for public office. It names a familiar habit of Greek and Roman civic life, and Paul forbids it to the assembly at 2:3.\f*, not purely, thinking to stir up trouble for me in my chains.
\p
\v 18 What of it? Only that in every way, whether in pretense or in truth, the Christ is announced — and in this I rejoice. Yes, and I will go on rejoicing,
\v 19 for I know that this will turn out for my rescue\f + \fr 1:19 \ft The words are Job's, exactly as the Greek scriptures render them: "and this will turn out for my salvation" (Job 13:16) — spoken by a righteous man suffering unjustly and certain he will be vindicated. The word can mean release from prison or final deliverance, and Paul leaves both open.\f*, through your petition and the supply of the Spirit of Jesus the Christ,
\v 20 in keeping with my eager expectation and hope that in nothing will I be put to shame, but that with all boldness, now as always, the Christ will be made great in my body, whether through life or through death.
\s1 Torn Between Staying and Departing
\p
\v 21 For to me, to live is Christ, and to die is gain.
\v 22 But if living on in flesh means fruit from my labor — then which I should choose, I do not know.
\v 23 I am pressed from both sides: I have the desire to be loosed \add [i.e. to cast off, the way a ship is untied from its mooring]\add* and to be with the Christ, for that is far, far better;
\v 24 but to stay on in the flesh is more needful for your sake.
\v 25 And being confident of this, I know that I will remain, and remain alongside you all, for your advance and joy in the faith,
\v 26 so that your boast in the Christ Jesus may overflow in me \add [i.e. so that you will have all the more to exult in, and my coming will be the occasion of it]\add*, through my coming to you again.
\s1 Live as Citizens Worthy of the Good News
\p
\v 27 Only — live as citizens\f + \fr 1:27 \ft Greek \tl politeuesthe\tl*, from \tl polis\tl*, the city: conduct your public life as members of a commonwealth. In a Roman colony, where citizenship was the possession people prized above all others, the word lands hard. Paul names the commonwealth he has in mind at 3:20.\f* worthy of the good news of the Christ, so that whether I come and see you or am away I may hear about you: that you are standing firm in one spirit, with one soul contending side by side for the faith of the good news,
\v 28 and startled\f + \fr 1:28 \ft The verb is the one used of a horse shying at a sudden noise.\f* by nothing from those who set themselves against you. This is a sign to them of destruction, but of your rescue \add [i.e. your steadiness in front of them is itself the evidence — of where they are headed, and of where you are]\add* — and this from God.
\v 29 For it has been given to you as a favor\f + \fr 1:29 \ft The verb is built on the same root as "favor" in 1:2 — Greek \tl charis\tl*. Both halves of what follows, the trusting and the suffering, are named as gifts.\f*, on behalf of the Christ, not only to trust in him but also to suffer on his behalf,
\v 30 having the same contest you saw in me and now hear to be in me.

\c 2
\s1 One Mind, and Others Counted Above Yourselves
\p
\v 1 If, then, there is any encouragement in the Christ, if any comfort of love, if any partnership of Spirit, if any tenderness of the inward parts \add [i.e. gut-level compassion]\add* and any compassion,
\v 2 fill up my joy: set your minds on the same thing, having the same love, of one soul together, setting your minds on the one thing.
\v 3 Nothing out of self-seeking rivalry, nothing out of empty glory\f + \fr 2:3 \ft Greek \tl kenodoxia\tl*, "empty reputation": the chase after standing with nothing behind it — the round of offices, patronage, and public benefaction by which honor was won and displayed in a Roman town.\f*, but in lowliness of mind\f + \fr 2:3 \ft Greek \tl tapeinophrosynē\tl*. To Greek and Roman ears this was no virtue at all: it named the cringing disposition proper to slaves and the low-born, while a free man aspired to greatness of soul. Paul makes it the governing disposition of the assembly.\f* counting one another above yourselves;
\v 4 each of you looking not to your own things, but each of you also to the things of others.
\s1 The Mind That Was in the Christ
\p
\v 5 Set your minds on this among yourselves, which was also in the Christ Jesus —
\v 6 who, being in the form\f + \fr 2:6 \ft Greek \tl morphē\tl*: not the look of a thing as opposed to what it really is, but the form in which what a thing is comes to expression. The same word returns in the next verse — the form of God, the form of a slave.\f* of God, did not count being equal with God a thing to be seized\f + \fr 2:6 \ft Greek \tl harpagmos\tl*, a rare word: either something snatched at that one does not yet have, or something already held that one refuses to exploit. The descent that follows fits the second. Adam was told "you shall be as gods" (Genesis 3:5) and grabbed; this one held the reality and let it go.\f* \add [i.e. something to clutch and work to his own advantage]\add*,
\v 7 but emptied himself \add [i.e. poured himself out, keeping back nothing of what the position gave him]\add*, taking the form of a slave\f + \fr 2:7 \ft A \tl doulos\tl* stood at the bottom of the world Philippi knew — no rights, no standing, no say. The wording also carries the figure of Isaiah 52:13-53:12, the servant of the LORD who is marred, despised, cut off, and afterward exalted.\f*, coming to be in the likeness of men. And being found in shape as a man,
\v 8 he brought himself low, becoming obedient as far as death — and death on a cross\f + \fr 2:8 \ft Crucifixion was the slave's death. Roman citizens were exempt from it by law, and Cicero called it the cruellest and most revolting of penalties. Israel's Torah added its own verdict: "cursed is everyone who hangs on a tree" (Deuteronomy 21:23). In a Roman colony, these words name the bottom of every ladder there was.\f*.
\p
\v 9 For this reason God also lifted him high above everything\f + \fr 2:9 \ft The wording echoes Isaiah 52:13, where the servant of the LORD "shall be exalted and lifted up and greatly glorified."\f* and gave him as a favor the name that is above every name\f + \fr 2:9 \ft In Israel that is the name of God himself — the four letters held too holy to pronounce, read aloud instead as "Lord" and written \tl Kyrios\tl* in the Greek scriptures. Verse 11 says the name outright.\f*,
\v 10 so that at the name of Jesus every knee should bend — of those in the heavens and on the earth and under the earth \add [i.e. every being there is, wherever it exists]\add* —
\v 11 and every tongue openly confess that Jesus the Christ is Lord\f + \fr 2:11 \ft Isaiah 45:23, where it is the LORD himself who swears "to me every knee shall bow, every tongue shall swear" — from the most uncompromisingly monotheistic passage in Israel's scriptures. In Philippi, "Lord" was also a title Caesar carried.\f*, to the glory of God the Father.
\s1 Work Out What God Is Working In
\p
\v 12 So then, my beloved: as you have always obeyed — not only when I was present, but now much more in my absence — work out your own rescue \add [i.e. carry it through to its finish, the whole of it]\add* with fear and trembling\f + \fr 2:12 \ft A fixed phrase in the Greek scriptures for the awe that falls on people who find themselves dealing with the living God (Exodus 15:16; Psalm 2:11). It is not cringing dread of him.\f*;
\v 13 for God is the one working in you, both the willing and the working, for the sake of his good pleasure.
\p
\v 14 Do everything without grumbling\f + \fr 2:14 \ft Greek \tl goggysmos\tl*, the word for Israel's grumbling in the wilderness (Exodus 16:7-8; Numbers 14:27) — not ordinary complaint but a refusal to trust the God who had just brought them out of Egypt.\f* and arguing,
\v 15 so that you may become blameless and unmixed, God's children without blemish in the middle of a crooked and twisted generation\f + \fr 2:15 \ft Deuteronomy 32:5, where those words describe Israel itself in the Song of Moses. What Israel there failed to be, this assembly is told to be — and the crooked generation is now the world around them.\f*, among whom you shine like lights\f + \fr 2:15 \ft Greek \tl phōstēres\tl*, the word for the lamps God set in the sky at creation (Genesis 1:14-16), and used of the wise who "shall shine like the brightness of the firmament" (Daniel 12:3).\f* in the world,
\v 16 holding out the word of life \add [i.e. holding onto it, and holding it up where it can be seen]\add* — so that I may have a boast on the day of the Christ, that I did not run for nothing or labor for nothing.
\p
\v 17 But even if I am being poured out as a drink offering\f + \fr 2:17 \ft The wine poured out beside the main sacrifice (Numbers 15:5-10; 28:7). It was the lesser, accompanying offering — which makes their faithfulness the sacrifice proper and his own life the libation poured over it.\f* upon the sacrifice and priestly service of your faithfulness, I rejoice — and I rejoice together with you all.
\v 18 And in the same way you also, rejoice, and rejoice together with me.
\s1 Timothy and Epaphroditus
\p
\v 19 But I hope in the Lord Jesus to send Timothy to you soon, so that I too may be of good heart when I know how things stand with you.
\v 20 For I have no one else of like soul, who will genuinely care about how things stand with you;
\v 21 for they all seek their own things, not the things of Jesus the Christ.
\v 22 But you know his proven worth — that as a child with a father he served as a slave beside me for the good news.
\v 23 So him I hope to send, as soon as I can see how things go with me;
\v 24 and I am confident in the Lord that I myself will come soon as well.
\p
\v 25 But I counted it necessary to send you Epaphroditus — my brother and fellow worker and fellow soldier, and your messenger\f + \fr 2:25 \ft Greek \tl apostolos\tl* in its ordinary sense: the man you sent.\f* and the one who served my need\f + \fr 2:25 \ft The word is \tl leitourgos\tl*, from the priestly service named at 2:17. Carrying money and food to a prisoner is counted here as an act of worship.\f* —
\v 26 since he was longing for you all and in distress, because you had heard that he was sick.
\v 27 For he was indeed sick, close alongside death. But God had mercy on him, and not on him only but on me also, so that I would not have sorrow upon sorrow.
\v 28 So I have sent him all the more eagerly, so that you may see him and rejoice again, and I may be less sorrowful.
\v 29 So welcome him in the Lord with all joy, and hold men like him in honor,
\v 30 because for the work of the Christ he came near to death, gambling with his own life\f + \fr 2:30 \ft The verb belongs to the gaming table: he threw the dice with his own life on it.\f* to fill up what was lacking in your priestly service toward me \add [i.e. not that you held anything back, but you could not be here yourselves, and he came in your place]\add*.

\c 3
\s1 Watch Out for the Cutters
\p
\v 1 For the rest, my brothers: rejoice in the Lord. To write the same things to you is no trouble to me, and for you it is safe.
\p
\v 2 Look out for the dogs\f + \fr 3:2 \ft "Dog" was the standing insult for the unclean outsider, the scavenger that ate what was thrown out. Paul turns it back on men who would apply it to uncircumcised Gentiles.\f*. Look out for the bad workers. Look out for the mutilation\f + \fr 3:2 \ft A wordplay with a knife in it. The word for circumcision is \tl peritomē\tl*, "cutting around"; Paul writes \tl katatomē\tl*, "cutting to pieces" — the term for the pagan gashing of the body that the Torah forbids (Leviticus 21:5; 1 Kings 18:28).\f*.
\v 3 For we are the circumcision \add [i.e. we are the covenant people that mark was always about]\add* — we who worship by the Spirit of God and boast in the Christ Jesus and put no confidence in flesh,
\s1 Everything on the Credit Side, Moved to the Loss Column
\p
\v 4 though I myself have confidence even in flesh. If anyone else thinks he has confidence in flesh, I more:
\v 5 circumcised on the eighth day \add [i.e. on the day the Torah names, as one born to it and no adult convert]\add*; out of the race of Israel; of the tribe of Benjamin\f + \fr 3:5 \ft Benjamin stayed with Judah when the northern tribes broke away (1 Kings 12:21), and it was the tribe of Israel's first king, Saul — whose name Paul bore.\f*; a Hebrew out of Hebrews \add [i.e. raised in the ancestral tongue and the ancestral ways, not in Greek ones]\add*; as to Torah, a Pharisee \add [i.e. of the strictest party there is about keeping it]\add*;
\v 6 as to zeal, chasing down the assembly\f + \fr 3:6 \ft "Zeal" named something specific: the violent action for God's honor that Phinehas took and that was credited to him as righteousness (Numbers 25:11-13), and that the Maccabees claimed as their model. Paul is describing what was, at the time, the summit of his covenant loyalty.\f*; as to righteousness in Torah, become blameless \add [i.e. no charge could be brought against me by the law's own measure]\add*.
\p
\v 7 But whatever things were gains to me, these I have counted loss because of the Christ.
\v 8 More than that, I count everything to be loss because of the surpassing worth of knowing the Christ Jesus my Lord — for whose sake I have lost it all, and I count it dung\f + \fr 3:8 \ft Greek \tl skybala\tl*: refuse, excrement, the scraps thrown out to the dogs. The word is coarse, and it is chosen — he has just called these men dogs.\f*, so that I may gain the Christ
\v 9 and be found in him, not having a righteousness of my own out of Torah, but the one through the faithfulness of the Christ\f + \fr 3:9 \ft The Greek can be read either as "faith in the Christ" or as "the faithfulness of the Christ" — his own. The second is taken here, because it is what 2:8 has just described: obedience as far as a cross.\f* — the righteousness out of God, resting on faithfulness —
\v 10 to know him, and the power of his rising, and the partnership of his sufferings, being shaped to the same form as his death \add [i.e. my own life taking the shape his took, down the same road]\add*,
\v 11 if somehow I may arrive\f + \fr 3:11 \ft The Greek is genuinely tentative. It is not doubt about the resurrection but about his own arrival at the end of a road he is still walking, as the next verses say outright.\f* at the rising up out from among the dead\f + \fr 3:11 \ft An intensified form of the usual word, found nowhere else — the bodily raising up out from among the dead that Daniel 12:2 describes.\f*.
\s1 Not Yet Arrived, Still Running
\p
\v 12 Not that I have already received it, or have already been brought to completion; but I keep chasing\f + \fr 3:12 \ft The same verb rendered "chasing down" at 3:6, where the object was the assembly. The pursuit has not slackened; what he pursues has changed.\f*, to see if I may lay hold of that for which I was also laid hold of by the Christ Jesus.
\v 13 Brothers, I do not reckon myself to have laid hold of it. But one thing: forgetting what lies behind and straining forward to what lies ahead,
\v 14 I chase toward the mark \add [i.e. the finish line, the one thing my eyes stay on]\add*, for the prize\f + \fr 3:14 \ft The victor's award at the games, handed out by the judge at the end of the race — not to everyone who entered it.\f* of the upward calling of God in the Christ Jesus.
\p
\v 15 So then, as many of us as are full-grown \add [i.e. and being full-grown means knowing you have not yet arrived]\add*, let us set our minds this way. And if in anything you set your mind otherwise, God will uncover this also to you.
\v 16 Only — whatever we have already reached, let us keep in line by it \add [i.e. keep step with it, the way a rank of soldiers holds its formation]\add*.
\s1 Two Ways of Walking, Two Ends
\p
\v 17 Become imitators together of me, brothers, and keep your eye on those who walk this way, just as you have us for a pattern.
\v 18 For many walk — of whom I have often told you, and now tell you weeping — as the enemies of the cross of the Christ.
\v 19 Their end is destruction, their god is the belly \add [i.e. appetite is what they actually obey]\add*, and their glory is in their shame; they set their minds on earthly things.
\p
\v 20 For our citizenship\f + \fr 3:20 \ft Greek \tl politeuma\tl*. Philippi was a colony of Rome: its people held Roman citizenship, lived under Roman law, and belonged to a capital most of them had never seen. Paul puts the assembly in exactly that position toward heaven.\f* is in the heavens, and out of it we are waiting eagerly for a rescuer\f + \fr 3:20 \ft Greek \tl sōtēr\tl*, "savior" — a standing title for emperors and civic benefactors, and God's own title in Israel's scriptures: "besides me there is no savior" (Isaiah 43:11).\f*, the Lord Jesus the Christ,
\v 21 who will refashion the body of our lowliness \add [i.e. these bodies of ours as they now are — mortal, worn, breakable]\add* into the same form as the body of his glory, according to the working by which he is able also to bring all things under himself.

\c 4
\s1 Stand Firm, and Settle the Quarrel
\p
\v 1 So then, my brothers — beloved and longed for, my joy and my wreath\f + \fr 4:1 \ft Not a royal crown but the victor's wreath at the games — what he expects to be wearing when the running of 2:16 is over.\f* — stand firm this way in the Lord, beloved.
\p
\v 2 I urge Euodia, and I urge Syntyche, to set their minds on the same thing in the Lord.
\v 3 Yes, and I ask you too, true yokefellow\f + \fr 4:3 \ft Greek \tl syzygos\tl*, "one yoked together with." Paul does not name him; the Philippians knew who was meant.\f*: take hold alongside these women, who contended side by side with me in the good news, together with Clement and the rest of my fellow workers, whose names are in the book of life\f + \fr 4:3 \ft God's register of those who belong to him (Daniel 12:1). In the passages where it first appears it is a register that can be written in and blotted out of: Exodus 32:32-33; Psalm 69:28.\f*.
\s1 Rejoice, Pray, and Set the Mind on What Is Good
\p
\v 4 Rejoice in the Lord always. Again I will say it: rejoice.
\v 5 Let your forbearance \add [i.e. your willingness to give way rather than press what is yours by right]\add* be known to all men. The Lord is near\f + \fr 4:5 \ft The word carries both senses at once: near in time, and near at hand. Israel's psalms use it the second way — "the LORD is near to all who call on him" (Psalm 145:18).\f*.
\p
\v 6 Be anxious about nothing, but in everything, by prayer and petition with thanksgiving, let your requests be made known to God.
\v 7 And the peace of God, which surpasses all understanding, will stand guard over \add [i.e. be posted around like a garrison]\add* your hearts and your thoughts in the Christ Jesus.
\p
\v 8 For the rest, brothers: whatever is true, whatever is weighty \add [i.e. carries dignity and asks to be taken seriously]\add*, whatever is right, whatever is pure, whatever is lovely, whatever is well spoken of — if there is any excellence\f + \fr 4:8 \ft Greek \tl aretē\tl*, the central word of Greek moral philosophy and the only place Paul uses it. He is reaching for the vocabulary his readers already had, and telling them that whatever in it is genuinely good belongs in their thinking too.\f* and if there is anything worthy of praise — take account of these things.
\v 9 What you also learned and received and heard and saw in me — practice these things. And the God of peace will be with you.
\s1 Enough, in Any Circumstance at All
\p
\v 10 But I rejoiced greatly in the Lord that now at last you have bloomed again \add [i.e. put out fresh growth, the way a tree does after a winter]\add* in setting your minds on me — indeed your minds were on it all along, but you had no opportunity.
\v 11 Not that I speak out of any lack, for I have learned, in whatever circumstances I am, to have enough\f + \fr 4:11 \ft Greek \tl autarkēs\tl*, "self-sufficient" — the Stoic word for the wise man who carries within himself everything he needs and is untouched by fortune. Paul uses the word and moves its source outside himself; see verse 13.\f*.
\v 12 I know how to be brought low, and I know how to overflow. In every circumstance and in all things I have been let into the secret\f + \fr 4:12 \ft The verb is the one used for initiation into the mystery cults, whose rites were disclosed to initiates only. What he has been let in on is stated plainly in the next verse.\f* — both to be fed full and to go hungry, both to overflow and to fall short.
\v 13 I have strength for everything in the one who makes me strong \add [i.e. the Christ, who keeps supplying it]\add*.
\s1 Your Gift Was a Sacrifice, and God Accepted It
\p
\v 14 All the same, you did well, sharing together in my pressure.
\v 15 And you Philippians yourselves know that in the beginning of the good news, when I went out from Macedonia, not one assembly entered into partnership with me in an account of giving and receiving\f + \fr 4:15 \ft A bookkeeper's phrase: debits and credits in a shared ledger. Paul is describing their support as a standing joint account between them.\f* — only you.
\v 16 For even in Thessalonica you sent, once and again, for my need.
\v 17 Not that I am after the gift; I am after the fruit that grows to your account \add [i.e. what is accruing on your side of the ledger, not on mine]\add*.
\v 18 I have received everything in full\f + \fr 4:18 \ft The verb that closes out a receipt in the business papyri: paid in full, the account settled.\f*, and I overflow. I have been filled, having received from Epaphroditus what came from you — a fragrance of sweet smell, a sacrifice received and well pleasing to God\f + \fr 4:18 \ft The standing phrase in the Greek scriptures for an offering God accepts (Genesis 8:21; Exodus 29:18; Leviticus 1:9). Money carried across the Aegean to a prisoner goes up like the smoke of an altar.\f*.
\p
\v 19 And my God will fill every need of yours according to his riches in glory in the Christ Jesus.
\v 20 To our God and Father be the glory into the ages of the ages. Amen.
\s1 Final Greetings
\p
\v 21 Greet every holy one in the Christ Jesus. The brothers with me greet you.
\v 22 All the holy ones greet you, and most of all those of Caesar's household\f + \fr 4:22 \ft Not the emperor's family but the imperial staff — the great body of slaves and freedmen who ran the empire's business, posted in every major city. Some of them now confess a Lord (2:11) other than the one whose house they serve.\f*.
\v 23 The favor of the Lord Jesus the Christ be with your spirit.
